  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Civil Society and Communication: Strategies to Consolidate Civil Society Participation, Paraguay</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Alter Vida was in existence since 1985 as a non-profit organisation. Its mission was &#x201c;to work for sustainable development, through the appropriate management of ecosystems, with the participation, coordination and commitment of all social actors&#x201d;. It then evolved into Radio FM Trinidad in 1994, as a result of meetings between Alter Vida and the local shantytown committees, women&#x2019;s groups and cultural organisations of the Trinidad District in Asunci&#xf3;n. CAFOD has supported Radio Viva since 1994. The aim of the radio station has been to find practical solutions to basic needs, as well as to promote solidarity, creativity, gender equality, social justice and cultural identity. 

The organisation and radio station aims to strengthen and increase the participation of civil society. Through the promotion and strengthening of an active civil society that engages government authorities on issues affecting it. Radio Viva aims to do this through radio programmes on its radio station aimed at the wider public and also strengthening other local radio stations in its network. Radio Viva seeks to encourage a critical unbiased press that voices the concerns of the citizens of Paraguay. Radio Viva provides radio programmes and a news letter in Guarani. Providing information and programming in Guaran&#xed; has meant that those who have been marginalized by their inability to speak Spanish (around 40% of the population) are now able to access information previously unavailable to them. This is integral to its objectives of promoting cultural identity and self-esteem in marginalised sectors of the population.

As the result of the election of a new president in April 2008, this project will focus on monitoring the government performance in areas of social policy such as health education and agrarian reform.  Radio Viva are also working together this year with three other CAFOD partners to develop common lines of action with a view to the development of a programme.</narrative>
  </description>
